title: "In Lieu of Flowers"
date: "2026-07-22"
excerpt: "On a warm Tuesday morning in the last week of June, at a mahogany writing desk in the parlor of a small red-brick colonial on Xavier Drive in Bardstown, Kentucky, a seventy-four-year-old retired ninth-grade English teacher named Rosemary Curran Ballard, Romie to everyone who has known her since Miss O'Bryan's freshman French at Nazareth Academy in the fall of 1965, sits down with a fresh Papermate and the yellow legal pad her husband Jim used to draft mash-bill supply requisitions on at Heaven Hill Distillery for forty years, and writes, in the plain steady hand of a woman who buried her husband the previous Monday morning at the Basilica of St. Joseph Proto-Cathedral, the twenty-three-word line at the bottom of his obituary that will run, on Thursday morning at four-fifteen, on the third floor of a small brick building on West Stephen Foster Avenue that has printed the *Kentucky Standard* twice a week since 1852. Somewhere on the fourth floor of a converted red-brick building on the campus of a small Catholic university in Louisville, on Monday morning at nine-oh-eight, the record for James Elliott Ballard, Bellarmine class of 1968, reads *lapsed since 2017, last gift $250.* Barbara Nally at Barlow-Bonnycastle Funeral Home has been typing the *in lieu of* line into the small Dell laptop on the front-desk return since 1993. The university does not read what Barbara types."
